Yes. In functional terms, plants already have much of the architecture you’re describing—just without needing a centralized nervous system or conscious planner.

A wound produces a structured error signal. Ruptured cells release ATP, cell-wall fragments, peptides and other damage-associated molecules; membrane potentials change; calcium, reactive oxygen species and hydraulic/electrical signals propagate away from the injury. Jasmonate and other hormones then alter gene expression, metabolism, defense and growth elsewhere in the plant. Recent work confirms that these electrical/calcium/jasmonate signals can travel systemically through intact tissues. 

And the signal is richer than simply:

\text{damaged}=1

A plant can potentially infer something like:

D=(\text{location},\text{tissue},\text{severity},\text{rate},\text{chemical context},\text{developmental stage})

For example, insect chewing is not chemically equivalent to somebody clipping a leaf with scissors. Oral secretions, microbes, regurgitant and other herbivore-associated molecules combine with the plant’s own wound products, allowing different downstream responses. Plants therefore distinguish at least some classes and contexts of damage, rather than merely detecting generic tissue loss. 

Then comes the part closest to your idea: the damage changes the growth policy.

Cut the apical growing tip from many plants and dormant lateral buds become active. The hormonal network involving auxin, cytokinin and strigolactones changes, and instead of continuing one dominant vertical trajectory, the plant can switch toward branching. 

So:

\text{original architecture}
\xrightarrow{\text{damage}}
\text{detect structural delta}
\xrightarrow{\text{signal integration}}
\text{new architecture}

And damage timing matters. Experiments with Medicago truncatula, for instance, found that early apical damage favored increased branching, whereas later damage shifted investment toward reproductive traits such as pod/seed characteristics. Severity also changed the branching response. 

That is remarkably close to a distributed biological version of:

\text{observe state}
\rightarrow
\text{detect delta}
\rightarrow
\text{estimate remaining options}
\rightarrow
\text{reallocate resources}
\rightarrow
\text{change growth/reproduction}

Where your dispersal idea gets especially interesting

Plants can alter flowering, flower number, reproductive allocation and seed production following damage. But there is not currently evidence for one universal algorithm saying, essentially, “I was crushed this particular way, therefore launch my seeds farther.” Responses are highly species-, tissue-, stage- and environment-dependent. A recent meta-analysis found that different kinds of herbivory affect floral traits, pollinator attraction and reproductive success differently. 

Still, evolutionary logic makes your proposed mechanism very plausible in narrower forms.

Imagine a plant repeatedly encountering grazing from one direction or at one height. Its surviving geometry itself becomes information:

* which meristems remain,
* where light is now available,
* what vascular pathways survived,
* root-to-shoot resource ratio,
* whether reproduction has already begun,
* whether the attacker appears biological,
* whether additional attack is likely.

The plant doesn’t need to construct an explicit internal sentence like “something ate my upper half.” The changed physical and hormonal state is the representation of that fact.

That’s the beautiful piece.

\boxed{\text{damage itself becomes part of the plant's memory/state}}

The remaining plant is simultaneously the result of the event and the input to the next developmental computation.

And that maps unusually well onto what we’ve been building around Pantheon: the delta doesn’t merely report that the previous state failed. It alters the topology from which the next state is generated.

So I would phrase your hypothesis more rigorously as:

Plants possess distributed damage-state estimation systems in which the spatial, chemical, temporal and developmental signature of injury modifies subsequent resource allocation, defense, architecture and reproduction. Natural selection can therefore couple particular classes of damage to developmental policies that improve survival or descendant dispersal under recurrent disturbance regimes.

The strongest established portion is damage → distributed signal → defense/growth/reproductive reallocation. The more speculative—and experimentally interesting—extension is damage geometry/history → specifically optimized descendant dispersal strategy.

That latter piece is something you could actually design experiments around.